::STAT_U02_L06_GuidedPractice_Q01::
Question ID: STAT_U02_L06_GuidedPractice_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.5
Question:
In a normal model, about what percent of values are within two standard deviations of the mean?
{
=A. About 95%#The empirical rule gives about 95% within two standard deviations.
~B. About 68%#About 68% is within one standard deviation.
~C. About 50%#Half the data is not the empirical-rule estimate for two standard deviations.
~D. About 99.7%#About 99.7% is within three standard deviations.
}

::STAT_U02_L06_GuidedPractice_Q02::
Question ID: STAT_U02_L06_GuidedPractice_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.6
Question:
A normal model has mean 100 and standard deviation 15. What interval contains about 68% of values?
{
~A. 70 to 130#This is two standard deviations from the mean.
~B. 100 to 115#This only includes values above the mean.
=C. 85 to 115#About 68% are within one standard deviation: 100 - 15 to 100 + 15.
~D. 55 to 145#This is three standard deviations from the mean.
}

::STAT_U02_L06_GuidedPractice_Q03::
Question ID: STAT_U02_L06_GuidedPractice_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.5
Question:
A normal model has mean 60 and standard deviation 8. About what percent is above 68?
{
~A. About 34%#About 34% lies between the mean and one standard deviation above.
=B. About 16%#68 is one standard deviation above the mean, so about 16% is above it.
~C. About 68%#68% is within one standard deviation, not above one standard deviation.
~D. About 84%#About 84% is below one standard deviation above the mean.
}

::STAT_U02_L06_GuidedPractice_Q04::
Question ID: STAT_U02_L06_GuidedPractice_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.6
Question:
A test score distribution is normal with mean 70 and standard deviation 5. About what percent is between 65 and 75?
{
=A. About 68%#65 to 75 is one standard deviation below and above the mean.
~B. About 95%#About 95% would be within two standard deviations.
~C. About 34%#About 34% is only one side from the mean to one standard deviation.
~D. About 16%#About 16% is one tail beyond one standard deviation.
}

::STAT_U02_L06_GuidedPractice_Q05::
Question ID: STAT_U02_L06_GuidedPractice_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.5
Question:
What does estimating a population percentage from a normal model require first?
{
~A. The data must be categorical only#Normal models are for numerical distributions.
~B. The standard deviation must be zero#A standard deviation of zero would mean no spread and is not a typical normal model.
~C. The distribution must have two separate peaks#Two peaks suggest a normal model may not be appropriate.
=D. The data should be reasonably fit by a normal model#Population percentage estimates rely on the model being appropriate.
}
